      "id": "cebel-i-selc",
      "end": 1599,
      "contributors": [],
      "provenance": "Pleiades",
      "associationCertaintyURI": "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/association-certainty/certain",
      "start": 1500,
      "details": "<p data-start=\"182\" data-end=\"369\"><em>Cebel\u00fc\u2019s-S\u0320elc, Cebel\u00fc\u2019l-L\u00fcbn\u0101n, Cebel-i Uk\u0101m</em>: These are three mountains contiguous with one another which, extending from south to north, appear as a single mountain range.\u00a0The southern one, which lies near \u1e62afed (modern Safed<em data-start=\"423\" data-end=\"438\">, </em>Israel), is <em>Cebel-i S\u0320elc</em>. It stretches northward and passes beyond the city of <em>D\u0131ma\u015f\u1e33</em> (modern Damascus, Syria). The people of that region call it <em>Cebel-i S\u00fcneyd</em>\u00a0[appears as\u00a0<em>Seb\u1e33ad</em> in one of the manuscripts of this work].\u00a0After this extension, the mountain that faces the west of <em>Ba\u2018albek</em> (modern Baalbek, Lebanon) is called <em>Cebel-i L\u00fcbn\u0101n</em> (Mount Lebanon).\u00a0Just as it passes beyond <em>Ba\u2018albek</em> and reaches the mountain lying to the east of <em>\u1e6crabl\u016bs-\u015e\u0101m</em> (modern Tripoli, Lebanon), that mountain is called <em>Cebel-i \u2018Akk\u0101r</em>\u2014for <em>\u2018Akk\u0101r</em> is the name of a fortress located on its summit, and by association it has given the mountain its fame.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"159\" data-end=\"548\">After it continues northward and passes beyond <em>\u1e6crabl\u016bs</em> (modern Tripoli, Lebanon), they give the name <em>Cebel-i Uk\u0101m</em> to the mountain that first reaches <em>\u1e24\u0131\u1e63nu\u2019l-Ekr\u0101d</em>, then <em>\u1e24im\u1e63</em> (modern Homs, Syria), then <em>\u1e24am\u0101</em> (modern Hama, Syria), <em>Sh\u012brez</em> (likely <em data-start=\"470\" data-end=\"486\">Shayzar?, Syria</em>), and the borders of <em>Ef\u0101miyye</em> (modern <em data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"544\">Apamea, Syria</em>).\u00a0Then, passing beyond that and continuing in extension, the mountain appearing opposite <em>Cebel-i Uk\u0101m</em> is called <em>Cebel-i \u015ea\u1e25\u015feb\u016b</em>, meaning that it is named in attribution to the village of <em>\u015ea\u1e25\u015feb\u016b</em>.\u00a0This mentioned mountain passes by <em>Ma\u2018arra</em>, <em>Serm\u012bn</em> (modern Serm\u012bn, Syria), and the western side of <em>Ba\u2018albek</em>, and becomes connected to <em>Cebel-i R\u016bm</em>.\u00a0Likewise, <em>Cebel-i Uk\u0101m</em>, extending northward, passes <em>Mehveb</em>, then through <em>S\u0320u\u0121\u016br</em>, <em>Bekk\u0101s</em>, and <em>\u1e32ay\u1e63er</em>, and reaches <em>An\u1e6d\u0101kiyye</em> (modern <em data-start=\"1115\" data-end=\"1132\">Antakya, Turkey</em>), where it terminates.\u00a0Opposite this, there is yet another mountain, and the <em>Nehr-i \u2018\u0100\u1e63\u012b</em> (the Orontes River) flows between the two of them.\u00a0[translation]</p>",
